lection of the appearance the  intermediate and then the advancement of  those individuals until we make it into  the next appearance  and um so this is um just  one of the main goals of breeding is to  improve that population from which we're  going to be deriving individuals  and then for the release we need to  derive those uh lines and those could be  ingredients uh or we we are developing  those in red lines to produce a hybrid  so uh those cultivar development is the  second phase here on the right  where we use multiple year  evaluation multiple environments we do  selection for individuals now not for  breeding values  and then we release individuals of  course we are cycling all of this is not  disconnected they are all completely  connected and we're cycling individuals  back as parents so we're using different  strategies for selecting those parents  and um  one aspect that we can change is in this  breeding equation is time right how much  does it take to complete a full  generation uh until we can recycle the  parents back and for that there have  been several strategies that have work  some as double haploids that have worked  in many species and today i'm going to  talk a little bit about speed breeding  which is another strategy that helps in  decreasing the time that it requires to  complete a full cycle so in this case it  means the spree breeding and nothing  else and just manipulating uh the light  uh the photo period and the temperature  uh to together with the nutrients so  that we can make the plants flower  faster so that we can make them um  shorter times  and uh in this example uh what we did is  we evaluated those feed breeding in oats  uh and we found that we could go from  111 days to harvest  until 79. so that means that we can  increase and put a few more generations  in a year  when we are  doing this in our context we are using  it as a single seed descent strategy so  that we can  get fixed lines faster but there are  other ways in 